From: derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn)
Date: Wed, 20 Jul 1994 06:28:31 -0500 (CDT)
Subject: [#94-7-167] If Knowledge Limits Learning

Robin Hanson:

> Actually, Lenat would say he already knows how to make CYC learn, and
> has demonstrated this with EURISKO.  He thinks the learning *rate*,
> however, is limited by the amount of knowledge a system already
> contains.  Hence the focus of CYC, and the title of this thread.

Yes, I wonder, though, exactly why Lenat thinks that a chunk of
knowledge about Abe Lincoln's hair added manually will increase
discovery rate in the future.  Presumably, as they have taken
a blank slate and laboriously calligraphed two million bits
of knowledge into it, some experimental confirmation of his
hypothesis should be quite feasible -- how fast could it discover
stuff with 10,000 knowledge units?  How fast can it discover
with 200 times as many?  Are we getting linear speedup?

Here's what I think he probably means:  The more knowledge you
have, the more you can learn by making only a small number of
inferential links.  With more things that potentially link
together, there should be more useful knowledge units making
linkages.

For speedup, though, since discovery processes like EURISKO
operate by considering potential knowledge units and measuring
the "usefulness" of the result, we get speedup only if
the ratio of found/searched goes up.  I don't see any reason
to expect that this is true.

Further, if Lenat really wants CYC to read, it seems to me that
a central focus of their effort has to be encoding knowledge
about reading....

As to "tabula rasa emergent magic AI", I think it's at least
possible that there is some kind of fundamental theory of
intelligent behavior that could be succintly formulated
and incarnated.  Perhaps all those unemployed physicists
could be unleashed on the problem.

derek
