From: price@price.demon.co.uk (Michael Clive Price)
Date: Wed, 20 Jul 94 07:45:19 GMT
Subject: [#94-7-168] If Knowledge Limits Learning 

Peter McCluskey:
>  In the context of single neuron learning, it seems a bit strange for
> Robin to call hand-crafted input representations built-in knowledge,
> since I normally think of that as guidance from an external teacher.

I thought that strange, too.

> But there has to be some built-in knowledge in the nervous system,
> either in the form of hard-wired connections or non-random initial
> weights, to explain the learning patterns we observe.

Agreed, biological nervous systems have built-in structures.  Whether
artificial systems will also require them, beyond some generalised,
low-level learning algorithm, to make sense of the world I'm not so
clear about.  For instance, it is generally accepted that humans have
less hard-wiring than many (all?) other animals.  Humans are, it
rumoured, also the most intelligent.  Perhaps there is a connection here
and we should push for less hard-wiring for UIMs, not more.

> And the fact that we don't know how to improve on 
> random weights only shows that it is a hard problem.

I don't see how this implies the former.  If anything I would have
thought that the reverse is implied.  We can start from random weights
in training a neural network on some task because the final trained
network, from a behaviourist point of view, often seems so insensitive
to the starting state.

>> I'm guessing that the same "local learning rules" will operate on
>> all scale networks then they contain no knowledge about the
>> environment or any species-specific information.
> 
>  Based on my experience with neural nets, I don't believe that
> learning rules scale up tractably.   [...]

Just to clarify.  I assume you are referring to the time it takes for
a neural network converge on a solution, which does not scale tractably.

The more complex the network the longer it takes for the network to
settle down from a randomised start.  The convergence seems to scale
intractably with network size.  I agree, which is why I'm adopting a
suggestion I heard in another forum and suggesting that one way around
this might be to train up a small network and then allow it grow larger
by, say, adding neurons at locations where the weights refuse to settle
down.  This way, I hope, the network responds to representational
challenges by network growth.  At no point do we have to train a large
randomised network from scratch.  New neurons are introduced into an
environment where most of their neighbours have already settled down. 
If this approach works then a human-level AI will naturally grow into
a more intelligent mechanism.  (Of course nature has found some other 
solution to the problem of training large networks and it may turn out
that adopting this approach, when we understand it, may be more 
productive.)

Mike Price                         price@price.demon.co.uk

