From: hnash@mason1.gmu.edu
Date: Wed, 20 Jul 94 09:58:03 -0400
Subject: [#94-7-169] If Knowledge Limits Learning

I've been following the discussion between Robin and Mike with 
great interest.  I share much of Mike's intuition about 
emergence and learning.  However, Robin makes a good case for 
the possibility that tabula-rasa learning might be fundementally 
lacking.  

Robin hypothesizes that, in order to learn much, a brain must 
begin with a substantial amount of knowledge.  Left to discover 
knowledge in isolation, a tabula-rasa would take eons to 
discover most common-sense knowledge.  

I'd like to hypothesize that every brain must remain ignorant 
about the structure of most of its knowledge.  Of course, a 
brain can utilize each chunk of its knowledge in some way, but 
most chunks cannot be articulated.  Most chunks must remain 
impossible to articulate simply because it takes many chunks of 
knowledge to articulate a single chunk.  This non-articulable 
knowledge is similar to the pattern of connection weights in a 
trained nueral network.  

Roughly speaking, Robin's hypothesis would prohibit AI's from 
eductating themselves.  My hypothesis would also prohibit people 
from educating AI's.  I agree with Robin that neither of the 
methods we've been discussing ("tabula-rasa AI" or "articulated 
AI") is certain to succede or to fail.  


