Mike Price writes:
>Robin, you seem to be missing the point.  I stand by both paragraphs. 
>....  I can only think that you are making a lot of
>assumptions about my unstated beliefs which I can't fathom.

Perhaps I've misunderstood you.  Let me try to summarize.  You
originally said that the limit to getting AI was hardware, because "the
limiting thing is searching through the learning algorithm space."  I
introduced this new thread with the title "If Knowledge Limits Learning"
to suggest there could be another limiting factor, namely knowledge.

Your response that I had neglected training by humans led me to believe
you accepted knowledge as a possible limiting factor.  So I tried to
point out how knowledge is also a limiting factor in a training
scenario.  But your recent response to Peter McCluskey that you aren't
convinced "artificial systems will also require ... built-in structures"
suggests that you don't accept the knowledge limit, that you seek what
Derek calls "tabula rasa emergent magic AI".  (I agree with Derek that
this is "at least possible", but consider it unlikely.)

>My "vision" has not been altered by your responses (so far) which, I
>have to say, seem rather tangential to my twice-expressed concerns that
>your learning time estimates of >100,000 system-years exclude training

My estimate turns out to be rather insensitive to this.  If an AI needs
only know one part in a thousand of our common sense knowledge to know
enough to be trained by humans, this only lowers my time estimate by a
factor of two.

My estimate remains large even with a different power dependence of
learning rate on knowldege.  If the learning rate is proportional to
knowledge to some positive power, the estimate is never less ten thousand
system-years.  If the power is one half, the estimate is twenty thousand
years.  If the power is two, the estimate is ten billion years.

For every factor of ten that Lenat has underestimated the amount of our
common sense knowledge (his estimates have increased with time), all these
estimates go up by a factor of ten.

Robin Hanson

