Hadon Nashwrites:
>Most chunks must remain impossible to articulate simply because it
>takes many chunks of knowledge to articulate a single chunk.

Your conclusion may be true, but it doesn't follow from your
assumption.  Consider a 3.9 Mbyte compiled program on a machine with 4
Mbytes memory.  Imagine the program includes a decompiler which can
produce readible source code from its own code, but that such source
code takes ten times as much space to store.  If this decompiler can
work on code chunks less than 10 Kbyte large, the program could
"articulate" all it's "knowledge", one chunk at a time. 

