Okay, I think I finally understand where Robin and I are coming from on
this AI training business.  Robin says:
> But your recent response to Peter McCluskey that you aren't
> convinced "artificial systems will also require ... built-in 
> structures" suggests that you don't accept the knowledge limit,
> that you seek what Derek calls "tabula rasa emergent magic AI".
> (I agree with Derek that this is "at least possible", but consider
> it unlikely.)

To be clear, yes, I believe in "tabula rasa emergent magic AI", although
a better phrase will have to found :-)  I agree it is possible and it
may even be unlikely, but I consider it far more likely to work than the
other, symbol processing approaches.  (BTW we should know soon whether
Lenat's CYC/symbol processing approach is going to work according to Dec
1991 Sci Am, pg 91.  Lenat predicts CYC to reach "critical mass" in
1994/5.  I bet it doesn't.  Anyone for a light-hearted wager?)

> If an AI needs only know one part in a thousand of our common sense
> knowledge to know enough to be trained by humans, this only lowers my
> time estimate by a factor of two.

Ah!  All is becoming clear.  You say that training can only begin after
the network has reached human-level complexity (~5.10^6 chunks).  No
wonder we were talking past each other.  This is where we part company -
 right at the beginning.  I say that training starts on an otherwise
blank network with "just" a low-level learning algorithm.  I envisage
a small network with some pleasure/pain signal being trained to respond
to simple inputs to do things like navigate through mazes, sort coloured
cubes out and such like - simple spatial/motor tasks with no language
requirement.  The trainer trains by reward and punishment.  A bit like
training your dog not to crap on the carpet.  Over time the cues
increase in complexity and the network responds by growing in complexity
and intelligence and formulating its own sub-goals - this is the
"emergent magic" bit - until it starts responding to simple commands
and, later, full blown English.  I expect that much of this early, pre-
linguistic training can be automated and run through very quickly.  Only
the later linguistic input requires human tutoring/monitoring (unless
symbolic AIs are available!).  This reduces the time estimate from
100,000s years to years? months?

Of course *after* the network understands enough to start online reading
then it can dispense with a human tutor - if we want (and we may well
*not* want).

