 That is the largest problem (symptom?) that I had in mind, but there
is also a need to prevent unrelated concepts from interfering with each
other. If you try to train one big backprop net, it will often spread
the information it uses to solve a task over much of the hidden layer,
when we need to focus training on a small number of new connections
to handle each new idea.

>The more complex the network the longer it takes for the network to
>settle down from a randomised start.  The convergence seems to scale
>intractably with network size.  I agree, which is why I'm adopting a
>suggestion I heard in another forum and suggesting that one way around
>this might be to train up a small network and then allow it grow larger
>by, say, adding neurons at locations where the weights refuse to settle
>down.  This way, I hope, the network responds to representational
>challenges by network growth.  At no point do we have to train a large
>randomised network from scratch.  New neurons are introduced into an
>environment where most of their neighbours have already settled down. 

 This probably bears some resemblence to a good solution, but I don't
think instability in weights is a good criterion. With properly scaled
inputs and a sophisticated training algorithm (better than plain
backprop), my experience indicates that weights usually settle down.
I am more concerned about the network learning noise or settling into
an overly simplistic solution.
